meta { title = "Iteration", description = "Reusable integer ranges and owned iteration cursors.", order = 11, group = "Library reference" }

Import range from std/iter when you want to store an integer sequence, pass it to a function or traverse it more than once. A Range stores its start, exclusive stop and step. It does not allocate an array or store a current iteration position.

h2 { "range" }

code { "range(stop: i64) -> Range" }
code { "range(start: i64, stop: i64) -> Range" }
code { "range(start: i64, stop: i64, step: i64) -> Range" }

The default start is zero and the default step is one. A negative step counts downward. The stop is excluded, and a step pointing away from the stop produces an empty sequence. Arguments are evaluated once, from left to right, when the Range is created. Traversal does not repeat their effects.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/iter" import Range, range
from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn sum(values: Range) -> i64:
  total = 0
  for value in values:
    total = total + value
  return total

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  evens = range(0, 7, 2)
  try println(format("{}", sum(evens) + sum(evens)))
  return ()
""" }

This displays 24. Each call to sum traverses 0, 2, 4 and 6 independently. Sharing a Range does not share an advancing cursor. Its three i64 fields are immutable values, so ranges can also be captured by functions or passed to supported GPU functions.

A zero step causes a checked arithmetic fault, even when start equals stop. Advancing past the i64 limits ends traversal without wrapping around. This is a runtime fault, rather than an expected-error channel to handle with try.

When used as a function value, range has its full three-argument signature. For example, a callback expecting fn(i64, i64, i64) -> Range can receive range directly.

h2 { "Range" }

Range is an immutable record with i64 fields start, stop and step. Reading its fields does not consume it. If you construct the record directly, for validates its step before traversal begins. The range function performs that validation at construction.

The allocation-free syntax for value in range(...) is also available without an import. Import std/iter.range to use the factory outside that loop syntax, or give the import an alias such as steps. Both traversal forms use the same exclusive stop and overflow rules.

h2 { "Iterator[T, E]" }

An Iterator owns a position in a sequence. T is the type of each item; E is the expected error that advancing the cursor can produce. Iterator[i64, never] produces i64 values without expected errors. Exhaustion is represented by Option.None, separately from the error channel.

Cursors have a single owner. Assigning one to another binding or passing it to an own parameter transfers ownership. A function that advances a borrowed cursor must declare a mut parameter. Pass cursors through explicit function parameters rather than capturing them implicitly in an ordinary function. The cursor's storage is opaque: use the iteration functions rather than constructing or reading its fields.

h2 { "iter" }

code { "iter[S: Iterable](values: S) -> Iterator[S.Item, S.Error]" }

Create a cursor at the beginning of a range. Each call creates an independent position. Creating a cursor does not traverse the sequence or allocate an array proportional to its length. Use a for loop over the Range itself when you do not need to retain an advancing cursor.

h2 { "Iterable" }

code { "Iterable.iterator(values: Self) -> Iterator[Self.Item, Self.Error]" }

Iterable describes a reusable source. Its associated Item type specifies each item, and Error specifies failures while advancing the returned cursor. Its iterator function creates an owned Iterator[Self.Item, Self.Error]. Range implements this trait with Item equal to i64 and Error equal to never. A generic function can accept S: Iterable and call iter(values) without knowing the source's concrete representation. Trait dispatch is resolved during compilation.

h2 { "next" }

code { "next[T, E](mut cursor: Iterator[T, E]) -> Option[T] ! E" }

Advance the cursor once. Option.Some contains an item; Option.None means that no items remain. Further calls after exhaustion also return Option.None. With E equal to never, next is infallible and needs no try. For a fallible cursor, handle its expected errors independently of matching the Option.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/iter" import range, iter, next, close
from "std/option" import Option
from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  cursor = iter(range(2, 7, 2))
  while true:
    match next(cursor):
      Option.None: break
      Option.Some {value}:
        try println(format("{}", value))
  close(cursor)
  return ()
""" }

This displays 2, 4 and 6 on separate lines. The cursor advances only when next is called.

h2 { "close" }

code { "close[T, E](own cursor: Iterator[T, E]) -> unit" }

Stop iteration and release the cursor's suspended state. Closing consumes ownership, so the binding cannot be used afterward. Close a partially consumed cursor when you have finished with it. Leaving its owning scope also cleans it up, including on faults and cancellation.

